at the University of Wisconsin.

74
00:04:02,280 --> 00:04:05,440
In practice, it very rarely works that way,

75
00:04:05,440 --> 00:04:10,880
it's certainly not that simple. So in this unorthodox, messy world in which we live,

76
00:04:10,880 --> 00:04:16,560
essentially there are two paths. Two roads diverged in a chamber of Congress.

77
00:04:17,280 --> 00:04:22,640
So one is the really easy path where essentially if everybody's on the same page, there's not a lot

78
00:04:22,640 --> 00:04:27,520
of bipartisan disagreement, there's not a lot of conflict, what happens is essentially both chambers

79
00:04:28,160 --> 00:04:32,880
use these expedited procedures. The other path, the path of most legislation that you actually

80
00:04:32,880 --> 00:04:37,040
read about in the paper and you hear about in the news is the stuff that's more controversial

81
00:04:37,040 --> 00:04:43,040
where there's more partisan disagreement, that stuff gets hard and very messy.

82
00:04:43,040 --> 00:04:45,120
We're going to follow the complicated path for now.

83
00:04:45,120 --> 00:04:48,080
So part one, coming up with it.

84
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:50,360
So the first thing is you have to come up with a bill.

85
00:04:50,360 --> 00:04:54,520
You have to have an idea and then you have to translate that idea into what's called

86
00:04:54,520 --> 00:04:55,520
legislative language.

87
00:04:55,520 --> 00:04:56,880
I talked to Andy Wilson.

88
00:04:56,880 --> 00:04:59,040
He's a former staffer on Capitol Hill.

89
00:04:59,040 --> 00:05:06,960
An actual bill that adds to or amends some part of the United States code of laws.

90
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:09,920
Members of Congress work with their staff to write bills.

91
00:05:09,920 --> 00:05:14,800
They can also get help if they need it from the nonpartisan office of legislative counsel,

92
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:17,360
experts on legal language and how to write laws.

93
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:21,040
So sometimes when you have the idea and you have it written, you take some time to gather up

94
00:05:21,040 --> 00:05:24,640
support for the bill as cosponsors before you introduce it.

95
00:05:24,640 --> 00:05:29,120
If you've got several members of Congress as co-sponsors to a bill, that's like a signal

96
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:33,760
that this bill has lots of support. Co-sponsors are just adding their name to it. They might not

97
00:05:33,760 --> 00:05:38,480
have even read the thing. Bills in the House can have a few co-sponsors. They can have hundreds of

98
00:05:38,480 --> 00:05:43,200
co-sponsors, which makes them many times more likely to become a law. And as to crafting a bill,

99
00:05:43,200 --> 00:05:50,240
I never knew this. Anybody can write one. Special interest groups, lobbyists, people who are raising

100
00:05:50,240 --> 00:05:54,680
money for your campaign, it just has to be proposed by a member of Congress.

101
00:05:54,680 --> 00:05:59,960
So once you, along with your staff, the Office of Legislative Council and anyone else you

102
00:05:59,960 --> 00:06:03,560
fancy, have written a bill, where does it go?

103
00:06:03,560 --> 00:06:07,920
In the House, you have to take it down to the clerk of the House, which is a team of

104
00:06:07,920 --> 00:06:13,520
people that sit near where the President gives the State of the Union address every year,

105
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:17,760
and you actually have to drop the physical piece of paper with the legislative language

106
00:06:17,760 --> 00:06:21,280
on it into a box and that box is called the Hopper.

107
00:06:21,280 --> 00:06:22,280
The Hopper?

108
00:06:22,280 --> 00:06:23,280
Yes.

109
00:06:23,280 --> 00:06:27,420
In 2003, they replaced their Hopper from the 1950s.

110
00:06:27,420 --> 00:06:28,860
It's just a wooden box.

111
00:06:28,860 --> 00:06:32,420
But the clerk takes the bill, gives it a number.

112
00:06:32,420 --> 00:06:33,820
It's in sequential order.

113
00:06:33,820 --> 00:06:40,760
So the first one in the new Congress is HR1, House Resolution 1 or S1, Senate Resolution

114
00:06:40,760 --> 00:06:41,760
1.

115
00:06:41,760 --> 00:06:46,740
And then it's assigned to the relevant committee based on whatever the topic of the bill is.

116
00:06:46,740 --> 00:06:52,460
So if it's a bill that has to do with foreign policy, it's directed to the Foreign Relations

117
00:06:52,460 --> 00:06:53,460
Committee.

118
00:06:53,460 --> 00:06:59,340
If it's got to do with foreign policy, it's referred to the Agriculture Committee and

119
00:06:59,340 --> 00:07:00,340
so on.

120
00:07:00,340 --> 00:07:04,100
Who is in charge of deciding what committee it gets assigned to?

121
00:07:04,100 --> 00:07:07,700
That is the Speaker of the House or the Senate Parliamentarian.

122
00:07:07,700 --> 00:07:09,260
Sometimes it even goes to more than one committee.

123
00:07:09,260 --> 00:07:10,260
All right.

124
00:07:10,260 --> 00:07:14,420
Written, dropped in the hopper, assigned a number and assigned to a committee.

125
00:07:14,420 --> 00:07:15,420
Right.

126
00:07:15,420 --> 00:07:24,260
committee. The house has 20 permanent committees, the Senate has 17, and there's

127
00:07:24,260 --> 00:07:27,980
also four joint committees which have members of both the house and the Senate.

128
00:07:27,980 --> 00:07:33,220
Committees are formed to have expertise in a particular area so that they can be

129
00:07:33,220 --> 00:07:37,020
the first line of review of a particular topic instead of the whole house

130
00:07:37,020 --> 00:07:43,100
having to look at every little thing. So it's both to have deeper expertise on a

131
00:07:43,100 --> 00:07:48,300
given topic when looking at the various proposals that come before Congress and to have a little

132
00:07:48,300 --> 00:07:55,580
bit better process and have a more wieldy way in which the Congress can do its business.

133
00:07:55,580 --> 00:07:57,680
So who gets to be on these committees?

134
00:07:57,680 --> 00:08:01,920
Before each new Congress, every two years, there's a committee committee who chooses

135
00:08:01,920 --> 00:08:04,120
who gets to be in the committees.

136
00:08:04,120 --> 00:08:08,660
But what's most important about committees is that whichever party has a majority in

137
00:08:08,660 --> 00:08:14,460
the House or the Senate also has the majority in every single committee.

138
00:08:14,460 --> 00:08:20,180
For example, in 2019, the Agriculture Committee in the House has more Democrats than Republicans,

139
00:08:20,180 --> 00:08:24,340
and the Agriculture Committee in the Senate has more Republicans than Democrats.

140
00:08:24,340 --> 00:08:30,460
So that's why, one of the reasons why, you know, the party that's in majority has so

141
00:08:30,460 --> 00:08:35,660
much power, because they really have the ability to set the agenda, to set the terms of the

142
00:08:35,660 --> 00:08:42,060
debate and to ultimately decide what is voted on and what is in committee and in the full

143
00:08:42,060 --> 00:08:43,980
House or the full Senate.

144
00:08:43,980 --> 00:08:46,900
These bills get read sometimes for the very first time.

145
00:08:46,900 --> 00:08:51,100
They get hearings, they're discussed, experts are called in to speak to the effects of a

146
00:08:51,100 --> 00:08:54,660
bill, they get marked up, amendments are added.

147
00:08:54,660 --> 00:08:55,660
Question.

148
00:08:55,660 --> 00:08:56,660
Yeah.

149
00:08:56,660 --> 00:09:00,260
It's about amendments to bills, the kinds of things that can be added.

150
00:09:00,260 --> 00:09:04,740
Did you see the speech that John Stewart gave the House Committee on that 9-11 first

151
00:09:04,740 --> 00:09:11,420
It'll get stuck in some transportation bill or some appropriations bill and get sent over

152
00:09:11,420 --> 00:09:17,900
to the Senate where a certain someone from the Senate will use it as a political football

153
00:09:17,900 --> 00:09:22,260
to get themselves maybe another new import tax on petroleum.

154
00:09:22,260 --> 00:09:25,180
Because that's what happened to us in 2015.

155
00:09:25,180 --> 00:09:26,380
What was he talking about?

156
00:09:26,380 --> 00:09:31,020
That line stuck out to me and I emailed Andy about it and he said that in the House, amendments

157
00:09:31,020 --> 00:09:34,300
to bills have to be quote, germane.

158
00:09:34,300 --> 00:09:38,380
That means that they are relevant to the bill at hand.

159
00:09:38,380 --> 00:09:41,680
But in the Senate, there is no germane rule.

160
00:09:41,680 --> 00:09:46,400
You can propose any amendments on any topic whatsoever to a bill.

161
00:09:46,400 --> 00:09:50,040
So if you're a senator and you can't get your own bill to the floor and it doesn't

162
00:09:50,040 --> 00:09:54,560
have a lot of support, one thing you can do is get someone in a committee to put the stuff

163
00:09:54,560 --> 00:09:58,760
you want as a writer to another totally irrelevant bill.

164
00:09:58,760 --> 00:10:04,440
At this time period, this process of committee is where most bills die.

165
00:10:04,440 --> 00:10:07,440
And it's not because people vote on them in the committee and they say, this is a bad

166
00:10:07,440 --> 00:10:10,160
bill, it's just they never get out of committee.

167
00:10:10,160 --> 00:10:13,880
So much time and effort, and it just dies on the vine.

168
00:10:13,880 --> 00:10:20,960
It can simply disappear into the committee ether and never be heard from again.

169
00:10:20,960 --> 00:10:25,600
And if a bill doesn't make it to the floor for a vote in a Congress, it's dead.

170
00:10:25,600 --> 00:10:29,120
You have to start from the beginning and then next Congress.

171
00:10:29,120 --> 00:10:32,400
90% of bills die in committee.

172
00:10:32,400 --> 00:10:36,680
Are committee members required to do anything, like give a bill a hearing?

173
00:10:36,680 --> 00:10:37,680
No.

174
00:10:37,680 --> 00:10:40,320
They can just put it in the endless to-do pile.

175
00:10:40,320 --> 00:10:46,120
All right, so what if by luck and determination and bipartisan support, a bill survives the

176
00:10:46,120 --> 00:10:50,640
committee process and the committee reports it out and says, this thing is ready for a

177
00:10:50,640 --> 00:10:51,640
vote?

178
00:10:51,640 --> 00:10:53,720
Is it then put on the calendar?

179
00:10:53,720 --> 00:10:54,720
Absolutely not.

180
00:10:54,720 --> 00:10:55,720
Away!

181
00:10:55,720 --> 00:10:59,840
In the House of Representatives, the Speaker of the House decides which bill to make it

182
00:10:59,840 --> 00:11:02,240
out of committee gets the floor for a vote.

183
00:11:02,240 --> 00:11:06,720
And in the Senate, it's the Senate Majority Leader who decides what gets voted on.

184
00:11:06,720 --> 00:11:10,960
Theoretically, can the Speaker of the House or the Senate Majority Leader just kill a

185
00:11:10,960 --> 00:11:13,160
bill by not letting it get to a vote?

186
00:11:13,160 --> 00:11:16,680
Up until now, I've been talking about the House process and the Senate process as being

187
00:11:16,680 --> 00:11:21,280
pretty much the same, but here we have a major difference.

188
00:11:21,280 --> 00:11:24,360
The Speaker of the House, yes, can kill a bill.

189
00:11:24,360 --> 00:11:27,720
But there is a rarely enacted check on that power.

190
00:11:27,720 --> 00:11:30,200
It's called a discharge petition.

191
00:11:30,200 --> 00:11:35,360
Or if a majority of members of the House sign a physical petition, a piece of paper, that

192
00:11:35,360 --> 00:11:40,160
bill then gets brought out of committee and onto the floor for debate.

193
00:11:40,160 --> 00:11:42,200
What's the check in the Senate?

194
00:11:42,200 --> 00:11:43,200
Nothing.

195
00:11:43,200 --> 00:11:45,560
There's no discharge petition in the Senate.

196
00:11:45,560 --> 00:11:50,560
If the Senate Majority Leader does not want your bill to get voted on, there will be no

197
00:11:50,560 --> 00:11:58,160
vote. The only check on this power is election. The American people voting in a new majority party.

